E('renaissance|the renaissance', 'The Renaissance was a cultural movement that revived interest in classical Greek and Roman learning.', 'It began in Italy in the 14th century and spread across Europe until about the 17th century.',
  'Its name means “rebirth” in French.', 'It started in wealthy Italian cities such as Florence, supported by families like the Medici.', 'Humanism, a focus on human potential and classical texts, was at its heart.', 'Artists used perspective, anatomy and light to make realistic work.', 'Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo and Raphael are its most famous artists.', 'The printing press helped spread its ideas quickly.',
  'It laid the groundwork for modern science, art and thought.')
E('leonardo da vinci|da vinci|leonardo|mona lisa|the last supper', 'Leonardo da Vinci was an Italian painter, inventor and scientist, the ultimate “Renaissance man”.', 'He lived from 1452 to 1519.',
  'He painted the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper.', 'The Mona Lisa hangs in the Louvre in Paris and is probably the most famous painting in the world.', 'His notebooks are full of designs for flying machines, tanks and diving suits.', 'He dissected bodies to draw remarkably accurate anatomy.', 'He wrote his notes in mirror writing, right to left.', 'He left many projects unfinished.',
  'He is the model of curiosity across art and science.')
E('michelangelo|sistine chapel|statue of david', 'Michelangelo was an Italian sculptor, painter and architect of the Renaissance.', 'He lived from 1475 to 1564.',
  'He carved the statue of David from a single block of marble between 1501 and 1504.', 'He painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome between 1508 and 1512, mostly standing, not lying down.', 'He later painted The Last Judgement on the chapel’s altar wall.', 'He designed the dome of St Peter’s Basilica.', 'He considered himself a sculptor first.',
  'He is often called the greatest artist of all time.')
E('medici|medici family|lorenzo de medici', 'The Medici were a banking family who ruled Florence and became major patrons of the Renaissance.', 'They were powerful from the 15th to the 18th century.',
  'Cosimo de’ Medici founded their political power in the 1430s.', 'Lorenzo “the Magnificent” supported artists like Botticelli and the young Michelangelo.', 'The family produced four popes and two queens of France.', 'Their bank was one of the most powerful in Europe.',
  'Without Medici money, much Renaissance art might never have existed.')
E('printing press|gutenberg|johannes gutenberg', 'Johannes Gutenberg was a German goldsmith who introduced movable-type printing to Europe.', 'He developed his press around 1440 in Mainz.',
  'He combined metal movable type, oil-based ink and a press adapted from wine presses.', 'His most famous work is the Gutenberg Bible, printed around 1455.', 'Movable type had earlier been invented in China and Korea.', 'By 1500, millions of books had been printed in Europe.', 'Printing made books cheaper and spread literacy.',
  'It powered the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution and the spread of ideas.')
E('reformation|protestant reformation|martin luther|95 theses|ninety-five theses', 'The Reformation was a 16th-century movement that split Western Christianity.', 'It is usually dated from 1517, when Martin Luther published his Ninety-five Theses.',
  'Luther, a German monk, attacked the sale of indulgences, which promised to reduce punishment for sins.', 'He argued that people are saved by faith alone and that the Bible is the highest authority.', 'He translated the Bible into German.', 'Other reformers included John Calvin and Huldrych Zwingli.', 'In England, Henry VIII broke from Rome to create the Church of England.', 'The Catholic Church responded with the Counter-Reformation.',
  'It created Protestant Christianity and reshaped European politics, often through war.')
E('henry viii', 'Henry VIII was King of England, famous for his six marriages and his break with the Catholic Church.', 'He lived from 1491 to 1547 and reigned from 1509.',
  'He wanted to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon because she had no surviving son.', 'When the Pope refused, he made himself head of the Church of England in 1534.', 'His wives’ fates are remembered as “divorced, beheaded, died, divorced, beheaded, survived”.', 'He closed the monasteries and took their wealth.', 'His children Edward VI, Mary I and Elizabeth I all ruled after him.',
  'His break with Rome changed England’s religion forever.')
E('elizabeth i|queen elizabeth i|elizabethan', 'Elizabeth I was Queen of England, the last Tudor monarch.', 'She lived from 1533 to 1603 and reigned from 1558.',
  'She was the daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn.', 'She never married and was called the Virgin Queen.', 'Her navy defeated the Spanish Armada in 1588.', 'Her reign saw a golden age of literature, including Shakespeare.', 'She had her cousin Mary, Queen of Scots, executed in 1587.',
  'The Elizabethan age is remembered as a high point of English culture and power.')
E('spanish armada', 'The Spanish Armada was a fleet sent by Philip II of Spain to invade England.', 'It sailed in 1588.',
  'It aimed to overthrow Protestant Elizabeth I.', 'English ships used fire ships to break up its formation off Calais.', 'Storms wrecked many ships as they sailed around Scotland and Ireland.', 'Only about half the fleet returned home.',
  'Its defeat boosted England’s confidence as a naval power.')
E('shakespeare|william shakespeare|hamlet|romeo and juliet|macbeth', 'William Shakespeare was an English playwright and poet, widely seen as the greatest writer in the English language.', 'He lived from 1564 to 1616, born in Stratford-upon-Avon.',
  'He wrote about 39 plays, 154 sonnets and several long poems.', 'His tragedies include Hamlet, Macbeth, Othello, King Lear and Romeo and Juliet.', 'His comedies include A Midsummer Night’s Dream and Twelfth Night.', 'Many plays were performed at the Globe Theatre in London.', 'He invented or popularised hundreds of English words and phrases.', 'His plays are performed more than those of any other playwright.',
  'Phrases like “to be or not to be” and “break the ice” come from him.')
E('christopher columbus|columbus|1492', 'Christopher Columbus was an Italian explorer whose voyages for Spain began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas.', 'He lived from 1451 to 1506 and first crossed the Atlantic in 1492.',
  'He was trying to reach Asia by sailing west.', 'He landed in the Bahamas on 12 October 1492.', 'His ships were the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María.', 'He made four voyages, but died believing he had reached Asia.', 'His governorship was brutal, and Indigenous peoples suffered terribly.',
  'His voyages began the Columbian Exchange, which transformed both hemispheres, and his legacy is hotly debated.')
E('vasco da gama', 'Vasco da Gama was a Portuguese explorer, the first European to reach India by sea.', 'He lived from about 1460 to 1524 and reached India in 1498.',
  'He sailed around the Cape of Good Hope at the tip of Africa.', 'He landed at Calicut on India’s west coast.', 'His route let Portugal build a trading empire in spices.',
  'He opened a sea route between Europe and Asia that changed world trade.')
E('magellan|ferdinand magellan|first circumnavigation', 'Ferdinand Magellan was a Portuguese explorer who led the first expedition to sail around the world.', 'The voyage lasted from 1519 to 1522.',
  'He sailed for Spain with five ships and about 270 men.', 'He found a passage around South America, now the Strait of Magellan.', 'He named the Pacific Ocean, meaning “peaceful”.', 'Magellan was killed in the Philippines in 1521.', 'Juan Sebastián Elcano led one ship and 18 survivors home.',
  'The voyage proved how large the Earth really is.')
E('age of exploration|age of discovery', 'The Age of Exploration was a period when European sailors explored and mapped much of the world.', 'It ran from the 15th to the 17th century.',
  'Portugal and Spain led the way, followed by England, France and the Netherlands.', 'New ships and navigation tools made long ocean voyages possible.', 'Explorers sought trade routes to Asia’s spices.', 'It led to colonisation of the Americas, Africa and Asia.', 'It caused immense suffering, including the Atlantic slave trade.',
  'It began truly global trade and reshaped the world map.')
E('columbian exchange', 'The Columbian Exchange was the transfer of plants, animals, people and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world.', 'It began after 1492.',
  'Potatoes, maize, tomatoes and chocolate went from the Americas to Europe, Africa and Asia.', 'Horses, cattle, wheat and sugar went the other way.', 'European diseases like smallpox killed a huge share of Indigenous Americans.',
  'It transformed diets, economies and populations worldwide.')
E('atlantic slave trade|transatlantic slave trade|slave trade|slavery', 'The transatlantic slave trade forcibly transported millions of Africans to the Americas.', 'It ran from the 16th to the 19th century.',
  'An estimated 12.5 million people were taken from Africa.', 'Many died on the brutal sea crossing called the Middle Passage.', 'Enslaved people worked on plantations growing sugar, cotton and tobacco.', 'Britain abolished the trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its colonies in 1833.', 'The United States ended slavery in 1865 with the 13th Amendment.', 'Brazil was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888.',
  'Its effects, including racism and inequality, are still felt today.')
E('copernicus|nicolaus copernicus|heliocentrism', 'Nicolaus Copernicus was a Polish astronomer who proposed that the Earth goes around the Sun.', 'He lived from 1473 to 1543.',
  'Most people then believed the Earth sat still at the centre of the universe.', 'His book On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres was published in 1543, the year he died.', 'He placed the Sun near the centre, with the planets orbiting it.', 'His model still used perfect circles, so it wasn’t fully accurate.',
  'The shift he started is called the Copernican Revolution.')
E('galileo|galileo galilei', 'Galileo Galilei was an Italian astronomer and physicist, a founder of modern science.', 'He lived from 1564 to 1642.',
  'He improved the telescope and used it to observe the sky from 1609.', 'He discovered four large moons of Jupiter, now called the Galilean moons.', 'He saw that Venus has phases, supporting the idea that planets orbit the Sun.', 'He studied falling objects and motion.', 'The Catholic Church tried him in 1633, and he spent his last years under house arrest.', 'He is said to have muttered “and yet it moves” about the Earth.',
  'Einstein called him the father of modern science.')
E('kepler|johannes kepler', 'Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer who discovered the laws of planetary motion.', 'He lived from 1571 to 1630.',
  'He showed that planets move in ellipses, not perfect circles.', 'His three laws describe how planets speed up and slow down.', 'He used the careful observations of Tycho Brahe.',
  'His laws helped Newton develop the theory of gravity.')
E('scientific revolution', 'The Scientific Revolution was a period when modern science emerged in Europe.', 'It is usually dated from 1543, with Copernicus, to the late 17th century, with Newton.',
  'People began testing ideas with experiments and mathematics.', 'Key figures include Copernicus, Galileo, Kepler, Descartes and Newton.', 'Scientific societies like the Royal Society were founded.',
  'It created the scientific method we still use today.')
E('isaac newton|newton|laws of motion|gravity', 'Sir Isaac Newton was an English mathematician and physicist, one of the most influential scientists ever.', 'He lived from 1643 to 1727.',
  'He described the law of universal gravitation and three laws of motion.', 'He set them out in his book Principia, published in 1687.', 'The story of an apple falling inspiring him may have some truth, but it never hit his head.', 'He showed that white light is made of a spectrum of colours.', 'He co-invented calculus, independently of Leibniz.', 'He built the first practical reflecting telescope.',
  'His physics explained the motion of everything from apples to planets for more than two centuries.')
E('thirty years war|thirty years’ war', 'The Thirty Years’ War was a devastating conflict fought mostly in central Europe.', 'It lasted from 1618 to 1648.',
  'It began as a struggle between Protestant and Catholic states in the Holy Roman Empire.', 'It grew into a wider power struggle involving France, Sweden and Spain.', 'Parts of Germany lost a third or more of their population.', 'The Peace of Westphalia ended it in 1648.',
  'Westphalia helped establish the modern idea of sovereign states.')
E('louis xiv|sun king|versailles', 'Louis XIV was King of France for 72 years, the longest reign of any European monarch.', 'He lived from 1638 to 1715 and became king at age four in 1643.',
  'He was called the Sun King.', 'He ruled as an absolute monarch; “L’État, c’est moi” (“I am the state”) is attributed to him.', 'He built the vast Palace of Versailles and moved the court there.', 'He fought many costly wars.',
  'He became the model of absolute monarchy across Europe.')
E('english civil war|oliver cromwell|cromwell', 'The English Civil War was fought between supporters of King Charles I and Parliament.', 'It lasted from 1642 to 1651.',
  'Parliament’s New Model Army won.', 'Charles I was executed in 1649.', 'Oliver Cromwell ruled as Lord Protector from 1653 to 1658.', 'The monarchy was restored under Charles II in 1660.',
  'It established that a monarch could not rule without Parliament.')
E('peter the great', 'Peter the Great was a tsar of Russia who modernised the country.', 'He lived from 1672 to 1725.',
  'He travelled through Western Europe in disguise to learn shipbuilding.', 'He founded St Petersburg in 1703 as his new capital.', 'He built a navy and reformed the army.', 'He made nobles shave their beards.',
  'He turned Russia into a major European power.')
